environment variable (`CALC_LICENCE`), returning
`"the calculator's licence is not installed: set CALC_LICENCE"` before the
process runs. No licence, environment variable or other preparation appears in
the binding, the glossary, the Word mappings or the `halve` entry of
`docs/architecture/calc-cli.yaml`, which declares only
`go run ./services/calc halve VALUE` from the host root with an integer
argument and an integer on stdout. Remove that invented precondition so the
when always runs the declared command and keeps its integer stdout as the
result.

## Required Changes

### 1. New file: `tests/calc/helpers/calc.go`
Create the suite's helpers package (the directory does not exist yet) holding
the shared invocation of the declared `halve` command. It runs the command as a
process from the host root and returns only what the command printed — it never
halves the number itself. The module is `fixture/conceptbodiesextra`, so the
import path is `fixture/conceptbodiesextra/tests/calc/helpers`. Write the file
with exactly this content:

```go
package helpers

import (
	"errors"
	"fmt"
	"os/exec"
	"strconv"
	"strings"
)

// hostRoot is the repository root as seen from the suite's test directory
// (tests/calc), which is where the calculator CLI's commands are declared to
// run from.
const hostRoot = "../.."

// Halve runs the calculator's declared halve command as a process with the
// whole number and returns the integer it writes on stdout.
//
// The command is the only thing that halves anything: this helper passes the
// number through untouched and reports back whatever integer the process
// printed. It requires no preparation beyond the declared command itself.
func Halve(value string) (int, error) {
	cmd := exec.Command("go", "run", "./services/calc", "halve", value)
	cmd.Dir = hostRoot
	out, err := cmd.Output()
	if err != nil {
		var exitErr *exec.ExitError
		if errors.As(err, &exitErr) && len(exitErr.Stderr) > 0 {
			return 0, fmt.Errorf("halve %s: %w: %s", value, err, strings.TrimSpace(string(exitErr.Stderr)))
		}
		return 0, fmt.Errorf("halve %s: %w", value, err)
	}
	printed := strings.TrimSpace(string(out))
	result, err := strconv.Atoi(printed)
	if err != nil {
		return 0, fmt.Errorf("halve %s: stdout %q is not an integer", value, printed)
	}
	return result, nil
}
```

Note: do not set `cmd.Stderr` when using `cmd.Output()` — `Output` fails with
`exec: Stderr already set`. The `*exec.ExitError` carries the captured stderr,
as written above.

